\begin_layout Standard
Every learning approach is subject to a trade-off between performance and
 explainability.
 The greatest success has been achieved with the help of neural networks
 (NN), which were also essential to the success of AlphaGo.
 Neural networks can be used to learn a mapping between states and actions
 even for extremely complex problems.
 From the point of view of explainability, a NN is a generic equation system
 which can create models by using automated statistical methods.
 The strategy is thus only implicitly contained in the model in the form
 of the current parameterisation.
 A created model will not only be regarded as a black box because of the
 often several thousand variables, the activation functions of the neurons
 of a neural network are too unintuitive to be understood by humans, even
 in the case of neuron networks of small size like the perceptron.
 
\end_layout

