reference "fig:grow-vs-full-method"

\end_inset

 .
 Building random trees to a certain depth can either be done in  full or
 grow mode, with the full method filling up all levels with operators and
 the grow method inserting terminal nodes by chance (50%), resulting in
 a much smaller branching factor and less complex formulas.
 In order to obtain as diverse a mix of new candidates as possible, both
 approaches are usually combined (half ramped mode).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Post-hoc Interpretability with Symbolic Regression
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fitness of candidates can be determined in several ways.
 In a symbolic regression on offline data, the regression error is used
 to calculate how well the formula can explain the data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fitness in a regression is the sum of the distance between the outouts
 of the data set and the predicted values.
 Both the mean average error (MAE) and the mean square error (MSE) are widely
 used distance measurements for this purpose.
 The MAE is widely used in GP, as it indicates the average deviation from
 the target solution and is therefore distance-true.
 The MSE squares the distance and thus is not distance-true, making it a
 less honest indicator.
 However, in the MSE, outliers and strong deviations are punished more severely,
 which also allowed the black box to be approached more quickly and more
 reliably.
 This also means that volatile solutions are punished more severely.
 
\end_layout

