\begin_layout Section
Trusting Domain Experts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The pinnacle of explainable intelligent approaches, human-written approaches
 represent xxx und stellen einen sicheren Ankerpunkt für Exklärbare Ansätze
 dar.
 The main reasons to trust an expert over a machine learning approach should
 be xxx verinnerlicht werden, considering that humans also have a long learning
 process behind them before they can be considered experts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
xxx
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Dieser lange Trainingsprozess (technische Sicht, Lernstrategie, Sprache
 als Bestandteil xxx)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The human brain, with almost 90 billion neurons, is not only far more complex
 than artificial neural networks, the architecture has been perfected in
 many areas in an evolutionary process lasting millions of years.
 This enables humans to learn a wide range of skills in a very diverse learning
 process, while being severely limited in time and exploration compared
 to machines.
 Image recognition, for example, is perfected in early childhood to such
 an extent that it hardly needs any explanation.
 The learning process is intrinsically continuously monitored by primal
 fears and drives, dangers are analysed and the learned models are thus
 endowed with a high degree of security.
 Humans are also as lazy as they can be and, instead of making an effort
 to find new concepts for a problem, they try to solve a problem with what
 they have already learned.
 Language, as well as the ability to speak, is learned as a child and at
 the same time describes everything that a person can understand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
The limits of my language mean the limits of my world.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
(Ludwig Wittgenstein, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Die Grenzen meiner Sprache bedeuten die Grenzen meiner Welt.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wittgenstein1953philosophical"
literal "true"

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Interpersonal communication and the ability to implement successful models
 in all people (such as the Pythagorean theorem) also avoids over- and underfitt
ing as much as possible.
 As a communication protocol, a language works because the experiences between
 people are similar enough to be learned and comparable.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Let's consider, for example, the game Puzzle as an NP-hard problem, where
 a whole picture has to be assembled from a pile of pieces.
 Most people know how to recognize colors, that you first have to turn puzzle
 pieces to the colored side and that you should use the picture on the box
 as a reference point.
 A popular tip for beginners is therefore that you should start with the
 edge of the puzzle.
 This is helpful, because the learning process in humans usually consists
 of the composition of many comprehensibly strung together steps.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An artificial neural network has to do without all these properties as far
 as possible.
 Through an adapted network architecture and the enormous computing power,
 a specialist savant can nevertheless be trained to master the given task.
 In contrast to humans, a neural network is dependent on random exploration,
 which can find bias-free policies, but represents a tradeoff to the familiar
 learning process of humans.
 Solutions that are searched for with the aim of minimal representation
 in a language space as defined above can therefore be interpreted by humans,
 but since the search process is also based on chance, the formulas found
 are also free of human bias - but they still often appear to the observer
 more as a random combination of various operators than as a logically abstracte
d solution to a problem.
 
