\begin_layout Standard
The Mountain car problem was introduced in 1990 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "mountaincarOriginal"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, where a car is supposed to drive up a mountain as fast as possible that
 is too steep for the car's engine.
 To reach the target, an agent has to learn to use the counter slope to
 take a swing.
 The state space is implemented in an unusual way (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "MC-observationspace"

\end_inset

), which makes it difficult to connect these values in a formula .
 Therefore in the area of AI such values are often normalized.
 For experiments in this thesis though, we assume that the developer did
 not normalize the values for his program and search for solutions without
 this intermediate step.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Domain Expert approaches
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the optimal solution strategy, the following intermediate objectives
 must also be considered:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Premature reversal.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The goal can be reached even without a maximum swing, especially during
 the last swing a lot of distance and therefore time can be saved.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Always accelerate.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Doing nothing instead of accelerating only makes sense at turning points,
 otherwise speed is lost.
 Most of the time such waiting can be replaced by accelerating in the opposite
 direction.
 This neutral action is often chosen by neural networks in uncertain borderline
 cases, because the weights are only slowly adapted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The initial action.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

At the starting position it is already clear whether the goal can be reached
 with two or three turns.
 How many swings are taken is decided at the start position, which is why
 it has an extremely large influence on the time required.
 This can be seen in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "table:MC_agents_rewards"

\end_inset

; the comparison operator decides on the first swing direction and worsens
 the result by more than 10 steps.
\end_layout

