\begin_layout Section
Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this thesis, familiarity was presented as an essential factor for creating
 interpretable policies.
 The implementation with the edit-distance between programming trees leads
 to results, which are structurally different from the classical GP, because
 parts of the expert policy were recognizable in the solutions.Only with
 simple expert solutions (3-6 nodes) the TED lost its effect quickly, because
 the difference to the regular distance became too small.
 Therefore fixed nodes in GP-trees were introduced as a solution, which
 must not be changed.
 More extensive extensions could focus even further on human thought patterns
 in the search.
 This could be the use of if statements to further subdivide problems, weighting
 of functions or an interpretable evaluation of edits.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Reliable statements or implications about robustness and real rewards cannot
 be made due to the small number of benchmarks tested.
 However, the experiments suggest that the regression error did not differ
 significantly from solutions of classical GP, which may be due to a general
 convergence of the regression error up to the outliers.
 The real rewards of the mountain car were not only very robust against
 misruns but often even exceeded the target solution.
 In order to obtain particularly secure solutions, there are also possibilities
 for expansion here, which, for example, evaluate the candidates additionally
 based on their deviation from the start policy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As early experiments should show, the TED can lose its protective effect
 if the changes overcome the tree size.
 This is especially true for Origin trees with few nodes or strongly abstracting
 structures (e.g.
 frequent use of if statements).
 In extreme cases, the TED often differs only slightly from node-counting
 distance dimensions.
 When the implicit protection of logical core structures by the TED is not
 sufficient, then logic relevant structures with a very high weight can
 be made robust against changes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Applicability of the approach yyy (war davor in Chapter 4, beim Vorstellen
 des Ansatzes)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the regression error of the expert policy is too large, it may deviate
 too much from the black box for an optimization to succeed.
 This would mean that the two approaches differ fundamentally.
 Improvements that can be found may show differences, but will most likely
 fall within a suboptimal range of the solution space.
 This is supported by a sparse state space.
 If the regression error does not become small enough despite a manageable
 initial difference, the target solution might be too complex to be approximated
 properly with a mathematical formula.
 This does not mean that a good GP candidate cannot be found, but it might
 require online training.
 As a black box a convergent approach should be preferred to a volatile
 one (like PSO) if possible, because a solution might be too specific.
\end_layout

